// Book Six, Chapter 3 (session 63). The week after landing, and Saturday at the Yards, April 1924.
// Opens with the business lead's week report, then one day phase of two actions out of three, then the Saturday night climax at the Yards, then a short dawn.
// The coven fronts the week and stands in the Yards: the lead reports, a family member can be sent ahead, and the family can come to the gallery. Names are always checked with in_circle().
// Reads b6.circle, b6.haven, b6.call, b6.coin, b6.walk, b5.charge. Sets b6.papers, b6.rudek, b6.company and b6.tallis.
// Uses send(), meet(), discover(), obtain(), chart(), power(), earn(). Ends with rest() and => next, which pauses until chapter 5 exists (the manifest entry is incomplete).
@chapter b6c03
@title The Second Shift
@book relics-lost-god
@version 1

# start
## The week
$ b6.papers = "shared"
$ b6.rudek = "bargained"
$ b6.company = "alone"
$ b6.tallis = "listened"
$ _lead = ""
$ _reader_name = "Dora Valer"
?if coven_size() > 0
  $ _lead = business_lead()
?end
?if _lead != ""
  $ _reader_name = name(_lead)
?end
~> week
---
$ do phase("day", 2)
It is Friday afternoon, and the sun is a white coin over the lake, and the town is at work. There are three things in the day that want doing, and strength for two of them, and a gold coin's invitation waiting for the second shift tomorrow night.
-> day

# week
?if b6.haven == "tower"
  The vaults over the Exchange have been fitted by a hundred men who were all told it was for a safe deposit company, and every one of them believed it.
?elif b6.haven == "keepers"
  The keeper house on the Near North Side has been, in six days, the busiest address in the ward. Dora has found a way to have a cellar bricked, a boiler installed, and a locksmith dismissed, all without anyone in the street having seen a single thing.
?else
  The brownstone on the South Side has a new lock on every door and a very old elm in the yard that nobody has been allowed to trim.
?end
?if _lead != ""
  {_reader_name} gives the week's account in the long front room, with a lamp and a pot of tea that nobody drinks. It is a short account and a dense one. The Exchange has been open six days and has taken eleven callers, three of whom were policemen, two of whom were not, and one of whom left a card that said only *Pell*. The ticker has run. The vaults have held. {pg(_lead, "He", "She", "They")} {pg(_lead, "has", "has", "have")} heard, from three sources that do not know each other, that the Yards are talking.
  ?if b6.call == "family"
    The house on the lake has sent a basket of hothouse grapes and a note, which is not a courtesy. It is a receipt.
  ?elif b6.call == "form"
    The house on the lake has sent a short, warm, perfectly civil note. It says that the house hopes that Mr. Collins found the evening instructive.
  ?elif b6.call == "alone"
    The house on the lake has sent nothing at all, which is, {_reader_name} says, the most interesting message that it could have sent.
  ?else
    The house on the lake has not forgiven the clerk. It has been politely, thoroughly and invisibly present in every room that you have entered since.
  ?end
?else
  Dora gives the week's account standing at the end of a long table, in a plain brown dress, with a ledger. It is short, and it is, she says, a good deal less than it ought to be, since she has had no one to tell her what the household needs.
?end
?if b6.coin == "melted"
  The coin is a bar of gold in the Exchange's safe. The card is not. It stands against the clock on the mantel, where you put it, and you have noticed that every person who has come into the room has looked at it and then looked away.
?elif b6.coin == "shown"
  The coin has gone round the table so many times that it is warm. Nobody has said what they think of it. Everybody has an opinion.
?else
  The coin lies in your coat, over the heart, and does not warm.
?end
<~

# day
:day
?if actions_left() == 2
  There is a quiet in the house, as in any house the day after a long journey, and the matter of the stranger is not yet a thing that anyone has a word for.
?else
  The afternoon has begun to go over, and the shadows of the elevated railway have come down across the street in long black bars.
?end
* Go through Dora's grandfather's book, at the keeper house [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> papers
  $ do act()
* Call on the Yards' day office, and the man who answers there [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> haskell
  $ do act()
* Send one of the family to watch the gate until Saturday [once] ?if actions_left() > 0 and coven_size() > 0
  ~> gate
  $ do act()
* Let the day end
  $ do end_phase()
?if in_phase() != ""
  -> :day
?end
-> saturday

# papers
## The grandfather's book
Dora has brought it from the cellar in both arms, wrapped in a sheet, and set it on the table as if it might otherwise leave. It is a tall, thin, soft backed book of lined paper, black with age, and it is the record of a man who kept a name for forty years across a sea and told no one why. The writing is a clerk's writing, small and slanted and as neat as print. Most of it is accounts. A hundred pages in, between a bill for coal and a note about a wedding, there is a drawing.
It is done in pencil, with great care, from memory. It is a head, with a cap of leaves, and the eyes shut. Under it he has written, in a hand that has slipped a little, *this is the thing on the coin. I have seen it only once. I have been told never to say where.*
$ do discover("the_six_rays")
It is the face. It is, beyond any doubt, the same face that is now lying in your coat or in the Exchange's safe or going round the table in the early light, and it was drawn in eighteen eighty nine by a man who had been told, by somebody, what to watch for.
:the_book
* Tell the family everything that is in it, and put it on the table
  $ b6.papers = "shared"
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  Dora is very quiet. It is not a thing that her family has done in a hundred years, to put a secret on a table. She says that her grandfather would not have liked it. She says that he would, you gather, have been very proud.
  ?if coven_size() > 0
    The family reads it aloud, a page each, in a long evening round the lamp, as a family will, and it is the nearest thing to a feast that the house has had in some time.
  ?end
* Keep it to yourself, and ask Dora to say nothing
  $ b6.papers = "close"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  She says that she will say nothing. She says it in the plain voice of a woman who has been saying it all her life, to everyone, about everything, and does not find it hard.
* Copy it, and send the copy to the hill, with the keepers there
  $ b6.papers = "hill"
  $ do earn(-1)
  $ collesanto += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  It goes by a steamship and a country postman and a boy on a mule. It will be in the crypt at Collesanto by midsummer, in the hands of people who have been keeping what you do not know for eighteen hundred years. They will not ask what it is. They will make a place for it.
<~

# haskell
## The Yards' day office
$ do meet("rudek")
$ do chart("chicago.yards")
The Union Stock Yard and Transit Company keeps a long brick office on Exchange Avenue, with a flag over it and a clerk in every window. At a desk in the largest, under a map of the whole of the country in a shade of green, sits Mr. Thomas Haskell, who is very large, very red, very friendly, and has the look of a man who has never in his life been refused. He rises, and takes your hand in both of his, and sits you in a chair that has been placed lower than his.
"Mr. Collins," he says. "I heard that you had come. I have heard it from everybody. I am a plain man, Mr. Collins, and I will say plainly that there is a gentleman who takes an interest in all that goes on in my yard, and that he would be very glad to know a friend of the firm. He does not come out in the daytime. He would like me to ask whether you would take supper."
He does not say the name, and he does not need to. He has the manner of a man who works for something very large, and a little afraid of it, and very well paid.
:the_day_office
* Pay the compliment, with an envelope, and say that you will be glad to meet any friend of the firm
  $ b6.rudek = "paid"
  $ do earn(-1)
  $ standing += 1
  He does not look in the envelope. It is, you gather, a courtesy that is very rarely shown, and he is moved, and is made at once a little more wary, as a man is who has been given something by a stranger and cannot see what it is for.
* Say that you will be glad to see him, and that you have a few questions of your own
  $ b6.rudek = "bargained"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing += 1
  He is a good deal relieved. He says that his friend is a plain man too. He does not say what the friend will think of a question.
* Tell him, quite pleasantly, that a man who sends for a stranger had better learn the stranger's habits first
  $ b6.rudek = "warned"
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing -= 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  The big red face loses a little of its colour. He is not a coward, and does not look like one. He looks like a man who has been told, in a courteous voice, a thing that he will have to repeat, word for word, to someone he does not like. He says that he will see to it.
<~

# gate
## The gate
:the_gate
* Fiammetta, who will see everything and be seen by nothing ?if in_circle("fiammetta") and member("fiammetta", "status") == "active" and not on_mission("fiammetta")
  $ do send("fiammetta", "scout", 1)
  Fiammetta puts on a plain brown coat and a man's cap, and goes out by the back, and is gone before the coat has finished swinging. She has said that she will find the second gate. She has said it in the tone of a woman who would be mortally offended if it did not exist.
* Thibault, who will listen ?if in_circle("thibault") and member("thibault", "status") == "active" and not on_mission("thibault")
  $ do send("thibault", "scout", 1)
  Thibault says that he will find the telephone line, and the boy who listens at it, and the man who pays the boy, and that he will be home by morning. He has the air of a man who has been given at last a toy that he understands.
* Hanno, who will count ?if in_circle("hanno") and member("hanno", "status") == "active" and not on_mission("hanno")
  $ do send("hanno", "scout", 1)
  Hanno will count the carts through the gate, and the men on the shift, and the number of shoes on the loading dock. He says that a place can be known by the number of its shoes. He is, in every case that you have ever tested it, perfectly right.
* No one, after all
  <~
