-> dawn

# gallery
## Over the pens
The foreman of last week has been told, and he does not say a word. He takes you up a long iron stair to a gallery that runs the whole length of the killing floor, thirty feet above the cattle, in a smell and a heat that are like a hand on the face. It is the place where the buyers stand, in the daytime, to watch the pens fill. At the far end there is a table, and a lamp, and a pot of coffee that has been steaming for an hour and has not been touched.
A tall man sits at the table in a gray overcoat, with his gloves on the cloth. He rises as you come.
He is pale in a way that is not the paleness of the dead. It is more like the paleness of a thing that has been kept out of the weather. He has a long, quiet, bony face, and gray eyes, and a courtesy that has a great deal of patience in it. He does not offer to shake hands. He says, "Mr. Collins. It is very good of you to come. I am so very glad that you did not melt it."
$ do meet("tallis")
$ do discover("the_tenants")
?if b6.coin == "melted"
  You say that you did. He smiles, in a way that is the very opposite of smiling. "Yes," he says. "I know. It does not matter. It is not made of the gold." He sets a second coin on the table, and it is a perfect twin to the first, with the same face and six rays and one red one. "There are always more. It is the idea that is wanted."
?end
?if b6.company == "family" and coven_size() > 0
  ?if gift_holder("shadows") != ""
    {name(gift_holder("shadows"))} has gone, without a sound, to the stair behind him, and is a shade among the other shades on the wall, and he does not look round. He knows. It is in the line of his shoulders.
  ?end
  ?if gift_holder("strength") != ""
    {name(gift_holder("strength"))} has put a hand on the iron rail of the gallery, and it has bent by an inch, quite slowly, in the heat. The man in gray looks at it, and then at you, and nods very slightly, as one who was told.
  ?end
?end
