# tallis
## The coin
He sits, and bids you sit, and pours you a cup of coffee that you do not drink. The pens below them have gone strangely quiet, a hundred yards of cattle with their heads lifted.
"I will say what I am, since you will ask," says Mr. Tallis. "I am not one of yours. I do not feed, and I do not sleep, and I have not aged since the year of the Armistice at Compiègne, which was not the one that you are thinking of. I am a tenant. I live in a house which was never mine, and I pay my rent in errands. There are a few of us. It is not at all a thing that I am proud of."
He turns the coin over, in a glove. He holds it so that you can see the six rays and the one that is red.
"This is your family's figure," he says. "I think that you have known it, since a cellar in Rome. It is a very old thing. It is older than your line, and older than the five, and it was made, before any of you, to say who the house is for. The red ray is the sixth. It is the one that is not on the others. I have been asked to find who holds it."
:the_tenant
* Listen to him, and hear what he has been sent to say
  $ b6.tallis = "listened"
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  He is very grateful, and shows it by being exact. In the autumn the University of Chicago will lift a thing out of the ground that has been in the ground since before the first temple. It will come to this city in a crate with a label that says *Oriental Institute*. It is a fragment. It is not large. He is not able to say what it is, because he does not, himself, know, and he has been very carefully told not to guess. He has been told that the five will come for it. He has been told that the maker of the sixth line will come for it, and that no one can say which will come first. "I should like it," he says, "to be you. I think that I should be very much easier in my mind."
* Press him, and ask the name of whoever sent him
  $ b6.tallis = "pressed"
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ do power("minor")
  You do not raise your voice. You do not need to. The whole of the gallery has gone very cold, and the lamp has leaned, and the iron under your hand has begun, very slightly, to bend. He does not flinch. He goes, if anything, a little more pale, and a little more patient, as a man might who had been told in advance what he would be asked and had been unable to prepare an answer. "I cannot say a name," he says. "I should like to. I have not got one. It does not speak in words. It is the house." He draws a long breath that he does not need. "I will tell you what I can. In the autumn the University of Chicago will lift a thing out of the ground. The five will come for it. So will the maker of your line."
* Take him, and hold him, and send for the family to take him home
  $ b6.tallis = "held"
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing += 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  He does not resist. He lets you take him by the coat and he walks down the gallery stair beside you like a man in a dream, and he says, in a perfectly reasonable voice, that you are of course free to do as you please, and that he will be very glad to answer any question that he can, in a more comfortable room. It is the most unnerving thing that has happened to you in a week. You have never taken a prisoner who was glad of it.
  In the end you do not need to take him anywhere. It is simply that you have said what you will do, and he has agreed to it, and the matter has the dreadful, orderly air of something that he has seen before, in another town, in another century, from the other side of a table.
  He tells you what he knows, in the long front room, at a table, at four in the morning. It is the same, but for the number of things that he is careful not to say: that in the autumn the University of Chicago will lift a thing out of the ground, that the five will come for it, that the maker of your line will come for it.
?if b6.coin == "kept" or b6.coin == "shown"
  $ do obtain("ray_coin")
?end
He says one thing more, as he rises. "You will be asked a good many things this year, Mr. Collins. You will be asked to be a good man and you will be asked to be a strong one. I do not envy you. I have not been asked to be either, for a very long time."
<~

# rudek_arrives
## The Yards house
It is a quarter to two, and the second shift is at its height. There is a sound on the stair, below, like a barrel being rolled down it, and then there is a very large man standing at the foot of the gallery with a coat that does not fit and a face like a ham.
Augustin Rudek is the size of a door. He is Bohemian, and was a butcher's son in Pilsen before he was a vampire, and has kept all the habits. He has six men with him, who are all of them as big as he is and all of them silent. He carries a cleaver, as another man might carry a cane.
"I heard," he says, "that there was a stranger who was making a night of it in my house."
$ do chart("chicago.yards")
?if b6.rudek == "paid"
  "You sent the envelope," he says. "That was good manners. I do not often see good manners. I thought that I should come and look at the man who has them."
?elif b6.rudek == "bargained"
  "You told Tom that you had questions," he says. "I have a great many myself. I thought that we might ask them in the same room."
?else
  "You told Tom," he says, "to learn your habits. I have come to learn them."
?end
The gray man has risen, quite calmly, and put on his gloves. He says to Rudek, in the same quiet voice, "I was just going." Rudek does not move. He does not look at the tenant. It is, you see with a cold start, as if the man in gray were not there at all. It is not that he cannot see him. It is that he has been taught, a long time ago, not to.
:the_yards_house
* Tell Rudek that you will see him in a more suitable place, on a more suitable day, and take your leave
  $ standing += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It is the voice of a man who has said it before, and Rudek hears it, and hates it, and respects it, and does not move for a long moment. Then he steps back, and lets you pass, with his six men on the stair, and none of them look at you. It is a victory and not a good one. You have not said that you will not take what he holds. You have said that you will be civil about it.
* Say that the Yards will be as safe with you as with anyone, and offer him your hand
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  He looks at it for a very long time. Nobody has offered it. He takes it at last, in a paw like a side of beef, and holds it a little too long, and lets it go. "I will think about it," he says. It is, you gather, the most gracious thing that he has said in ten years.
* Let him see what the sixth line is, and let the cattle go quiet again
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ do power("major")
  $ standing += 2
  $ secrecy -= 1
  It is not a thing that you decide. The whole of the building has gone silent, and the pens, and the machines, and the great slow engines of the killing floor, and in the silence there is nothing at all but the sound of your own breath and of his, and he has taken, very slowly, one step back. He has been big all his life. He has not, until now, been small. He does not forgive it. He will not forget it either.
?if b6.company == "family" and coven_size() > 0
  ?if gift_holder("charm") != ""
    {name(gift_holder("charm"))} has stepped between, smiling, and said something about the cleaver, and Rudek, who has not been teased in twenty years, has very nearly laughed.
  ?end
?end
<~

# dawn
## The end of the shift
$ do discover("the_yards_house")
The whistles go at seven. The long line of men comes out of the gate with their aprons over their arms and their faces gray, in the early light, and goes off in knots along the cold brick street. It is a thing that happens every morning in this city, and it will happen tomorrow, and the next day, and in a hundred years.
You walk back toward the house in the gray. You have been given a name, and a warning, and a coin, and an enemy, and a thing that you will have to do before autumn that no one has been able to describe to you. There is a smell of coffee and horse dung and coal smoke. A boy is selling the morning paper at the corner and shouting something about a murder in the Loop.
?if coven_size() > 0
  The family is waiting at the end of the street. You do not have to tell them anything. They have been listening, for an hour, to the sound of the Yards, and they have heard it go quiet and then loud again, and they know.
?else
  Dora is waiting at the door in her good gray hat. She has been up all night, and does not say so. She says that there is a boiled egg, and that it is a long time since she was afraid for anyone, and that she was.
?end
$ do rest()
=> next
